# 1. Home Network Basics

A home network allows multiple devices to communicate with each other and access the Internet.

A typical home network consists of:

- Internet Service Provider (ISP)
- Modem (if separate)
- Wireless Router
- Wired Devices
- Wireless Devices

Example devices connected to a home network:

- Desktop computers
- Laptops
- Smartphones
- Smart TVs
- Gaming consoles
- Printers
- Security cameras
- Smart home devices

---

## Advantages of a Wireless Home Network

Wireless LAN (WLAN) offers several benefits:

- Mobility
- Easy installation
- Scalability
- Flexibility
- Lower installation cost
- Reliable communication

---

## Router Ports

Most home routers contain two main types of ports.

### Internet (WAN) Port

- Connects the router to the ISP.
- Receives Internet connectivity.

### LAN Ports

- Connect local wired devices.
- Used for computers, printers, and switches.

Most wireless routers also include:

- Wireless antennas
- Built-in Wireless Access Point (AP)

---

# 2. Network Technologies in the Home

Home networks use both wired and wireless technologies.

## Wired Network

Uses Ethernet cables.

Advantages:

- Faster
- Stable connection
- Lower latency
- Less interference

Common cable:

- Cat5e
- Cat6
- Cat6a

Ethernet cables contain four twisted wire pairs that reduce electrical interference.

---

## Wireless Network

Wireless communication uses electromagnetic radio waves.

Common frequency bands:

- 2.4 GHz
- 5 GHz

### 2.4 GHz

Advantages:

- Longer range
- Better wall penetration

Disadvantages:

- Slower
- More interference

---

### 5 GHz

Advantages:

- Higher speed
- Less interference

Disadvantages:

- Shorter range

---

## Bluetooth vs Wi-Fi

| Wi-Fi | Bluetooth |
|--------|-----------|
| Longer range | Short range |
| Higher speed | Lower speed |
| Internet access | Device-to-device communication |
| Uses 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz | Mainly uses 2.4 GHz |

---

# 3. IEEE 802.11 Wireless Standards

Wi-Fi networks follow the IEEE 802.11 standard.

Different versions include:

- 802.11b
- 802.11g
- 802.11n
- 802.11ac
- 802.11ax (Wi-Fi 6)

Newer standards provide:

- Faster speeds
- Better efficiency
- Improved range
- Support for more connected devices

---

# 4. Important Wireless Router Settings

## Network Mode

Determines which Wi-Fi standards the router supports.

Examples:

- 802.11b
- 802.11g
- 802.11n
- Mixed Mode

Mixed Mode allows devices using different standards to connect.

---

## SSID (Service Set Identifier)

The SSID is the name of the wireless network.

Examples:

- HomeWiFi
- OfficeNetwork
- MyRouter

All devices must use the correct SSID to connect.

---

## Channel

A Wi-Fi channel determines the frequency used for communication.

Changing channels can reduce interference from nearby wireless networks.

Most routers automatically choose the best channel.

---

## SSID Broadcast

SSID Broadcast controls whether the Wi-Fi name is visible.

Enabled:

- Devices can easily find the network.

Disabled:

- Users must manually enter the SSID.

Note: Hiding the SSID does **not** provide strong security by itself.

---

# 5. Setting Up a Home Router

Basic setup process:

1. Connect the router to the ISP.
2. Connect a computer using an Ethernet cable.
3. Open the router's configuration page.
4. Configure the Wi-Fi name (SSID).
5. Set a strong password.
6. Enable WPA2 or WPA3 security.
7. Save the settings.
8. Connect wireless devices.

---

## DHCP

Most home routers include a DHCP server.

DHCP automatically assigns:

- IP Address
- Subnet Mask
- Default Gateway
- DNS Server

Without DHCP, devices would need manual network configuration.

---

## Guest Network

Many routers allow the creation of a Guest Network.

Benefits:

- Visitors get Internet access.
- Guest devices cannot access personal devices.
- Improves network security.

---

## MAC Address Filtering

MAC Address Filtering allows only approved devices to connect.

Advantages:

- Extra layer of security

Disadvantages:

- Time-consuming to manage
- MAC addresses can be spoofed

